    <main>
    

	<h1>About IANA</h1>

	<p>For the Internet to work as a single seamless network, technical
		identifiers must mean the same thing everywhere: network identifiers
        like domain names and IP addresses must be assigned to a single party
        and have the same meaning globally, and communication protocols
        must use codes that have the same interpretation by every software implementation.
		The Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) maintains the authoritative
        public registries that record these allocations and definitions,
        providing a common global point of reference.</p>

	<p>Although the Internet is decentralized by design, a limited set of its
        technical elements require this globally coordinated administration.
        The IANA role is a public service. The registries we maintain are
        openly published, our services are provided without charge, and we act
        as a neutral steward implementing policies developed by the communities
        we serve. Much of our work involves processing requests for identifiers
        arising from technical standardization processes or submitted directly
        by users of IANA services. We assess each request carefully against
        the established policies governing the identifier’s allocation and use.</p>

	<p>Our responsibilities extend beyond maintaining the registries themselves. We
        also operate the systems used to publish the registries and their
        associated data, together with supporting operational services. These include
        infrastructure and events involved in producing, securing, and distributing
        key aspects of the DNS.</p>

    <p>The need for the IANA functions dates to the record-keeping activities that
        accompanied the earliest experiments leading to today’s Internet, and their
        origins can be traced to the 1970s. Today, the functions are performed by
        <a href="https://pti.icann.org/">Public Technical Identifiers</a>, a
        purpose-built affiliate of <a href="https://www.icann.org">ICANN</a> dedicated
        exclusively to delivering the IANA services.</p>

